{"causes": ["The dynastic strategy of William I, Duke of Berg, to divide his territories among his sons in 1395 led to William VIII's investiture as Count of Ravensberg.", "The complex succession laws and territorial ambitions within the Holy Roman Empire necessitated careful marital alliances and territorial distributions by ruling houses.", "The ongoing competition between major regional powers like the Duchies of Gelre and Cleves influenced the strategic value and political maneuvering around Jülich-Berg-Ravensberg."], "effects": ["William VIII's death without direct male heirs in 1428 allowed the County of Ravensberg to revert to his brother, Adolf, Duke of Jülich and Berg.", "The reunification of Ravensberg with Jülich-Berg under Adolf strengthened the overall territorial power and administrative coherence of the House of Jülich.", "His rule maintained the continuity of administration in Ravensberg, contributing to regional stability before its absorption into a larger ducal union."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "william-vii-of-jlich-1st-duke-of-berg", "sourceName": "William VII of Jülich, 1st Duke of Berg", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "william-viii-of-jlich-count-of-ravensberg", "targetName": "William VIII of Jülich, Count of Ravensberg", "context": "William VII, as William I, Duke of Berg, divided his territories in 1395, leading to William VIII becoming Count of Ravensberg."}, {"sourceSlug": "william-viii-of-jlich-count-of-ravensberg", "sourceName": "William VIII of Jülich, Count of Ravensberg", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "great-schism-western-schism", "targetName": "Great Schism (Western Schism)", "context": "William VIII's life and rule from 1382 to 1428 occurred during the tumultuous period of the Western Schism, which divided the Catholic Church."}, {"sourceSlug": "william-viii-of-jlich-count-of-ravensberg", "sourceName": "William VIII of Jülich, Count of Ravensberg", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "holy-roman-empire", "targetName": "Holy Roman Empire", "context": "William VIII's entire political career and territorial rule were situated within the geopolitical framework of the Holy Roman Empire."}, {"sourceSlug": "william-viii-of-jlich-count-of-ravensberg", "sourceName": "William VIII of Jülich, Count of Ravensberg", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "adolf-duke-of-jlich-berg", "targetName": "Adolf, Duke of Jülich-Berg", "context": "William VIII's death without male heirs in 1428 led to the reversion of Ravensberg to his brother Adolf, Duke of Jülich and Berg."}, {"sourceSlug": "duchy-of-jlich-berg", "sourceName": "Duchy of Jülich-Berg", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "william-viii-of-jlich-count-of-ravensberg", "targetName": "William VIII of Jülich, Count of Ravensberg", "context": "William VIII's County of Ravensberg was a constituent territory that would eventually be reunited into the larger Duchy of Jülich-Berg."}], "places": [{"name": "Düsseldorf, Germany", "role": "Parental Seat of Power"}, {"name": "Ravensberg, Germany", "role": "Primary Seat of Rule"}, {"name": "Cologne, Germany", "role": "Significant Regional Center"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: William VIII of Jülich, Count of Ravensberg", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "william-viii-of-jülich-count-of-ravensberg"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q92176", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_VIII_of_J%C3%BClich,_Count_of_Ravensberg"], "tags": ["Germany", "Religious Figures", "Europe", "comparative-religion", "doctrine-development", "cultural-diffusion"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "William VIII of Jülich, Count of Ravensberg is remembered as a landmark person of the Medieval era, referenced in 0 Wikipedia language editions. Key lasting impacts include: Shaped religious practice and spiritual communities; Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "William VIII's rule as Count of Ravensberg was crucial in maintaining regional stability and his death without heirs solidified the reunification of the County with the powerful Duchy of Jülich-Berg, shaping the political landscape of the Lower Rhine for centuries.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-24T07:26:05Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28082160349", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "William VIII of Jülich, Count of Ravensberg was the youngest son of William VII of Jülich, 1st Duke of Berg and Anna of the Palatinate.", "newValue": "William VIII of Jülich, Count of Ravensberg, born in 1382, was a pivotal, if sometimes overshadowed, figure in the complex dynastic politics of the Lower Rhenish-Westphalian Circle during the late medieval period. As the youngest son of William VII of Jülich, who became William I, Duke of Berg, and Anna of the Palatinate, his life was inherently tied to the territorial ambitions and succession challenges of the powerful House of Jülich. His primary significance stems from his brief yet impactful rule over the County of Ravensberg and his role in the eventual reunification of the Jülich-Berg territories under his brother, Adolf, following a period of fragmentation.

William VIII formally inherited the County of Ravensberg in 1395, a strategic territory east of Berg, as part of his father William I's efforts to consolidate and distribute his extensive holdings. This arrangement, however, was not without tension, as the long-term goal for the House of Jülich was to maintain and expand a unified dominion. His rule was characterized by the ongoing political maneuvering within the Holy Roman Empire, particularly concerning the Wittelsbach and Luxembourg dynasties, and the need to secure the borders and economic interests of Ravensberg amidst competing regional powers. Despite his relatively short tenure, William VIII contributed to the administrative stability of Ravensberg.

Upon William VIII's death on December 1, 1428, without direct male heirs, the County of Ravensberg reverted to his elder brother, Adolf, Duke of Jülich and Berg. This reunification was a crucial step in strengthening the combined Duchies of Jülich-Berg-Ravensberg, which would become one of the most significant territorial powers in the western Holy Roman Empire for centuries. His death thus inadvertently solidified the dynastic succession plan, preventing further fragmentation and consolidating the family's influence in a crucial period leading up to the Burgundian wars and the rise of the Habsburgs."}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-24T07:26:05Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28082160349", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Educated at in the 500 – 1500 CE", "type": "Person", "year": "500"}, {"title": "Influenced by in the 500 – 1500 CE", "type": "Person", "year": "500"}, {"title": "Born into in the 500 – 1500 CE", "type": "Person", "year": "500"}], "newValue": ["The dynastic strategy of William I, Duke of Berg, to divide his territories among his sons in 1395 led to William VIII's investiture as Count of Ravensberg.", "The complex succession laws and territorial ambitions within the Holy Roman Empire necessitated careful marital alliances and territorial distributions by ruling houses.", "The ongoing competition between major regional powers like the Duchies of Gelre and Cleves influenced the strategic value and political maneuvering around Jülich-Berg-Ravensberg."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-24T07:26:05Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28082160349", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Founded through William VIII of Jülich, Count of Ravensberg's historical significance", "type": "Person"}, {"title": "Wrote through William VIII of Jülich, Count of Ravensberg's historical significance", "type": "Person"}, {"title": "Established through William VIII of Jülich, Count of Ravensberg's historical significance", "type": "P